<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Specification</title></head><body><pre> Enhanced Functional Specification Document - Taschenrechenner - Grundrechenarten-Modul (Enhanced Version)

Overview
This module is designed to implement the fundamental arithmetic operations (Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, Division, Square Root, and Logarithm). Its purpose is to create a reusable library for internal projects that requires basic and advanced arithmetic functionalities.

2. User Stories
As a developer, I want to be able to easily integrate the Grundrechenarten-Modul into my project, so that I can save time and effort in implementing basic and advanced arithmetic operations.
As an end-user, I want the module to accurately perform arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, square root, and logarithm), so that I can rely on the results for my calculations.
As an end-user, I want the module to prevent division by zero, as well as handle errors for other types of invalid inputs, such as non-numerical or out-of-range values, so that I avoid mathematical errors and inconsistencies in my calculations.

3. Functional Requirements
SHALL correctly perform addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, square root, and logarithm operations, given valid input data.
SHALL provide a clear and concise error message for divide-by-zero errors, as well as other types of invalid inputs.
SHALL support decimal values and handle large numbers as needed.
SHALL implement input validation checks to ensure that the input data is always within a reasonable range and in the expected format.
MAY support other advanced mathematical functions as future requirements.

4. Functional Requirements (continued)
SHALL support unit testing examples for developers to easily test the integration.
SHALL be designed to support future integration with external APIs or services, as needed.

5. User Workflows
Primary use case: Performing arithmetic operations
The user calls the appropriate arithmetic function (e.g., add, subtract, multiply, divide, squareRoot, or log) of the Grundrechenarten-Modul, passing it the required input data.
The module performs the specified arithmetic operation using the provided input values, and validates the input data to ensure it is valid.
The module returns the result of the operation, along with any appropriate error messages, if any errors occurred during the operation or the input validation.

6. UI/UX Considerations
The module should have a user-friendly API for easy integration.
The error messages for various types of invalid inputs should be clear and concise, and provide suggestions for correcting the invalid input data.

7. Data Requirements
Input data: Two numbers for addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and square root operations. One number and a base (for logarithm). The input data should be validated to ensure it is within a reasonable range and in the expected format.
Output data: The result of the performed arithmetic operation. The module should support decimal values and handle large numbers as needed.

8. Integration Points
The Grundrechenarten-Modul is intended to be integrated into various internal projects. No external APIs or services are required at this time, but the module should be designed to support future integration with external APIs or services, as needed.

9. Constraints and Assumptions
The module assumes that the input data is always a valid numerical value (integer or decimal).
The module does not handle complex numbers or scientific calculations at this time.

10. Open Questions
Are there any specific advanced mathematical functions that should be prioritized for future implementation?
What is the preferred format for the input data (e.g., string, float, integer)?
This functional specification document aims to provide a clear understanding of the Grundrechenarten-Modul's purpose, requirements, and intended usage, as well as the improvements made to ensure a more robust and reliable module.</pre></body></html>